      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "improve: make timing-sensitive tests faster",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nSeveral timing-sensitive tests spend real wall-clock time waiting for debounce, hover, reconnect, and lifecycle windows. The waits add more than 13 seconds to focused Control UI feedback and about half a second to the managed-process test file.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nUse Playwright's controlled page clock for six browser timing windows, keeping the tested delays intact without sleeping. End the parallel managed-child fixture explicitly once its listener invariant has been observed instead of waiting for natural process exit.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo runtime or user-visible behavior changes. Developers get materially faster test feedback with the same assertions and timing semantics.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Same-box before/after profiling for the six optimized UI assertions: 25.38 s -> 12.25 s (51.7% faster).\n- Same-box managed-child file profiling: 635.0 ms -> 114.7 ms (82% faster).\n- Final rebased head: managed-child file 9/9 passed in 145 ms (`run_f7e1f8582f6f`).\n- Final rebased head: six focused UI assertions 6/6 passed in 13.86 s (`run_f3fee8e10569`).\n- Broad changed-surface gate passed (`run_49ead66732aa`).\n- Fresh autoreview produced no accepted/actionable findings; its sole report overlooked an existing `page.clock.install()` in the same test before navigation.\n\nAI-assisted: yes.\n",
